Convenient technologies often prioritize ease of use over security, leading to vulnerabilities that can be exploited by malicious actors.
When users opt for convenience, they often unknowingly surrender control of their data to third parties, increasing the risk of breaches and misuse.
Short-term convenience can create a false sense of security, making individuals less likely to adopt protective measures in the long run.
The erosion of trust in institutions due to convenience-driven data practices can lead to systemic issues in digital governance and accountability.
History shows that prioritizing convenience without considering security has resulted in major data scandals and loss of public confidence.